# Largest Divisible Subset

**Problem:** [LeetCode 368](https://leetcode.com/problems/largest-divisible-subset/)

## Approach: Dynamic Programming (LIS Pattern)

Sort the array in ascending order. Use a DP array to store the length of the largest divisible subset ending at each index.

Maintain a `prev_index` array to track the previous element in the subset and reconstruct the answer.

For every pair of indices `j` and `i`, if `nums[i] % nums[j] == 0`, we can extend the subset ending at `j`.

Update the DP state if a longer subset is found. Finally, reconstruct the largest divisible subset using `prev_index`.

## C++ Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> largestDivisibleSubset(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(begin(nums), end(nums));

        int n = nums.size();

        vector<int> dp(n, 0);
        vector<int> prev_index(n, -1);

        int last_index = 0;
        int maxL = 0;

        for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if(nums[i] % nums[j] == 0) {
                    if(dp[i] < dp[j] + 1) {
                        dp[i] = dp[j] + 1;
                        prev_index[i] = j;
                    }

                    if(dp[i] > maxL) {
                        maxL = dp[i];
                        last_index = i;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        vector<int> res;

        while(last_index != -1) {
            res.push_back(nums[last_index]);
            last_index = prev_index[last_index];
        }

        return res;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** O(n²)
- **Space Complexity:** O(n)

## Example

Input: `nums = [1, 2, 4, 8]`

Output: `[1, 2, 4, 8]`

Explanation: Each element is divisible by the previous element, so the entire array forms the largest divisible subset.
